==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|révolter|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|it|en}} {{term|rivoltare|lang=it}}, itself either from {{term|ri-|lang=it}} with the verb {{term|voltare|lang=it}}, or possibly from a {{etyl|VL.|en}} *''revoltāre'' < *{{term||revolvitāre}}, for *''revolūtāre'', frequentative of {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|revolvō||roll back}} (through its past participle {{term|revolūtus|lang=la}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|En-us-revolt.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|əʊlt|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|revolts|revolting|revolted|revolted}}

# To [[rebel]], particularly against authority.
#: ''The farmers had to '''revolt''' against the government to get what they deserved.''
#* Shakespeare
#*: Our discontented counties do '''revolt'''.
# To [[repel]] greatly.
#: ''Your brother '''revolts''' me!''
#* Burke
#*: This abominable medley is made rather to '''revolt''' young and ingenuous minds.
#* J. Morley
#*: To derive delight from what inflicts pain on any sentient creature '''revolted''' his conscience and offended his reason.
# To cause to turn back; to roll or drive back; to put to flight.
#: {{rfquotek|Spenser}}
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To be disgusted, shocked, or grossly offended; hence, to feel nausea; used with ''at''.
#: ''The stomach '''revolts''' at such food; his nature '''revolts''' at cruelty.''
# To turn away; to abandon or reject something; specifically, to turn away, or shrink, with abhorrence.
#* Milton
#*: Still '''revolt''' when truth would set them free.
#* J. Morley
#*: His clear intelligence '''revolted''' from the dominant sophisms of that time.

